<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joan I of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1273">1273</a>–<a href="/years/1305">1305</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joan I was Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and Countess of Champagne from 1274 until her death in 1305, the last of the house of Champagne. Her marriage in 1284 to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a> brought both lands under the French crown, and she founded the College of Navarre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Her three sons all became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Navarre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-i-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Joan I of Navarre" width="320" height="558" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joan_I_of_Navarre,_Queen_of_France_as_Benefactress,_from_a_portal_in_the_College_de_Navarre,_Paris,_c._1305,_limestone_-_Bode-Museum_-_DSC03464.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
